hance","p_rec":"If Stockton's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Stockton has set <strong>43 standing record lows</strong>, fewer than the 74 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">175 record highs</strong>, far more than the 74 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (71). Stockton's all-time high is 115\u00b0F, on July 23, 2006.","t_chg":"Stockton's days and nights both warmed. Days warmed faster.","p_chg":"Across 125 complete years: Stockton's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.15\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.09 to +0.20), about +1.9\u00b0F since 1893. Stockton's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.50\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.44 to +0.57), about +6.6\u00b0F since 1893.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 6 of 12 months. Highs rose in all 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Stockton's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.14 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 13.4 inches a year. The wettest year was 1983 (26.65 in); the driest, 2013 (4.59 in). Stockton almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 11 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}